<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Cisrhenian Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1797 CE – 1797 CE</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Brief project for a Rhineland sister republic of France, proclaimed in 1797 but abandoned within months.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After French armies occupied the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, local supporters of the Revolution in towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-cologne">Cologne</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-aachen">Aachen</a> proclaimed a Cisrhenian Republic in September 1797, with the encouragement of General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazare-hoche">Lazare Hoche</a>, who favoured a separate state allied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic never had a functioning government or capital; it consisted of declarations, liberty trees and a movement led by young intellectuals, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-gorres">Joseph Görres</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a>. Its supporters wanted independence from the princes and from direct French rule, but most of the population showed little support.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazare-hoche">Hoche</a> died in September 1797, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-campo-formio">Treaty of Campo Formio</a> the following month opened the way for French annexation. The left bank was organised into French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> from 1798, ending the project; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-gorres">Görres</a> later became a leading German Catholic publicist.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/cisrhenian-republic.jpg" alt="Cisrhenian Republic" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Blackcat · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Cisrhenian_Republic.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1797 CE – 1797 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cisrhenania, Cisrhenanische Republik</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1797&amp;focus=cisrhenian-republic" aria-label="Cisrhenian Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4838 411 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cisrhenian Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5158 667L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5158 667h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5158 667h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1797&amp;focus=cisrhenian-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
